📊 Generative Gaussian Classification: Linear vs. Quadratic BoundariesThis project explores the fundamental differences between Linear Discriminant Analysis (LDA) and Quadratic Discriminant Analysis (QDA) using Gaussian Generative Models. We analyze how data distribution, specifically outliers and variance, dictates whether a decision boundary should be a straight line or a complex curve.📝 Project OverviewWe implemented a classification system that assumes data follows a Gaussian (Normal) distribution. By testing two distinct datasets, we demonstrate how mathematical constraints on "shape" (covariance) change how a machine "sees" and separates groups.Key Objectives:Implementing Maximum Likelihood Estimation (MLE) for mean and variance.Visualizing the shift from Linear to Quadratic boundaries.Analyzing the impact of extreme outliers on model behavior.🛠 Techniques & Math1. The Linear Case (binclass.txt)In this experiment, we forced the model to act as a Rigid Observer. By fixing the covariance to an Identity Matrix ($I$), we assumed both classes were perfect, identical circles.The Result: Because the "shapes" of the groups are identical, the complex squared terms in the math cancel out, leaving a perfectly straight line boundary.Outlier Behavior: Even with "rebel" points far from the center, the boundary refuses to bend, maintaining a clean but uncompromising separation.2. The Quadratic Case (binclassv2.txt)Here, we allowed the model to be flexible. We calculated the specific shape and spread for each group based on the actual data points.The Result: The inclusion of extreme outliers (like at $(20, -30)$) stretched the red group significantly. Since the two groups no longer had the same shape, the math generated a curved (Quadratic) boundary.Impact: The boundary "wraps" around the clusters, adapting to the "messiness" of the data for a more accurate fit.📊 Quick ComparisonFeatureDataset 1 (Linear)Dataset 2 (Quadratic)AssumptionClasses are identical circles.Classes have unique shapes.BoundaryStraight Line.Curved/Bent.Outlier HandlingIgnores them to stay straight.Bends to accommodate them.MathQuadratic terms cancel out.Quadratic terms remain non-zero.⚙️ How to RunPrepare Data: Ensure binclass.txt and binclassv2.txt are in your /content/ directory.Dependencies:Bashpip install numpy matplotlib scikit-learn
Execute:Run the provided Python script to generate the decision boundary plots and print the feature summaries.🔬 Mathematical Proof (Briefly)The decision boundary occurs where the probability of being in the "Red" class equals the probability of being in the "Blue" class.When shapes are equal ($\Sigma_1 = \Sigma_2$): The $x^2$ terms disappear $\rightarrow$ Line.When shapes are different ($\Sigma_1 \neq \Sigma_2$): The $x^2$ terms stay $\rightarrow$ Curve.
